Prior to microscopic analysis, the samples were progressively ground using SiC papers with mesh sizes of 80, 120, 320, 600, and 1200. This sequential grinding process was followed by fine polishing with diamond paste to achieve a final surface finish of 1 μm, minimizing surface artifacts and ensuring accurate microstructural observation. For graphite characterization, the specimens were examined in the polished condition without chemical etching. In contrast, matrix phase identification required controlled etching, performed with a 5% Nital solution for 4 seconds to clearly reveal phase boundaries. The prepared samples were subsequently examined using optical microscopy. For each experimental condition, at least three specimens were analyzed, and 5–10 randomly selected fields of view (FOVs) per specimen were evaluated at a magnification of 100×, corresponding to a total analyzed area exceeding 25 mm 2  per condition. This statistical approach was adopted to ensure the reliability and representativeness of the quantitative microstructural data. Graphite nodule characteristics, including graphite type, nodularity ratio, and nodule count per square millimeter, were quantitatively determined using ImageJ software in accordance with ASTM A247-10. 24  Graphite particles with an equivalent circle diameter smaller than 10 µm were excluded to eliminate image noise and non-representative features. Nodular graphite was identified based on a shape factor greater than 0.6, providing a consistent criterion for morphology classification. All reported values are expressed as mean ± standard deviation. Based on the XRD analysis, the volume fraction of retained austenite at room temperature and the corresponding carbon content in the austenitic phase were calculated using Equations  1 – 3 , which are widely accepted in the literature for such evaluations. 4 , 25 , 26  However, it should be emphasized that the lattice parameter-based estimation of carbon content provides an approximat